21:32:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Painkillers usually **reduce pain signals**; they don’t make your body unable to detect injury. A few reasons this can feel different: - **They may not be fully active yet.** Many oral painkillers take 20–60 minutes to start working and longer to reach their strongest effect. - **The dose may not be enough for a new, sharp injury.** A sudden cut, bump, burn, etc. can produce a strong burst of pain signals that “breaks through” the medication. - **Different pains respond differently.** - Anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen work best when inflammation is a major cause of pain—like a sprain, toothache, or sore muscle. - Acetaminophen/paracetamol changes pain processing in the brain but doesn’t completely block sharp injury pain. - Even stronger prescription pain medicines reduce pain but generally don’t eliminate it. - **Pain involves attention and alarm systems.** A new injury is unexpected and your nervous system treats it as important, so it can feel very intense even if medication is already lowering your baseline pain. Also, taking pain medicine *before* a planned painful event can sometimes reduce pain afterward—for example, doctors use “pre-emptive analgesia” around surgery—but it still won’t make someone pain-proof. Don’t take extra doses to try to prevent injury pain; follow the label or a clinician’s advice, especially with acetaminophen/paracetamol or NSAIDs, which can be harmful in excess.
