21:33:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why you’d trust someone with decades in the gym. But experience can pass along practices that feel helpful without proving they’re safest or most effective. Taking ibuprofen/Advil before a big lift or game does **not prevent injuries** and does not reliably block the pain of a new injury. It may reduce delayed soreness or an already-inflamed ache, but it can also make it easier to ignore early warning signs and keep loading tissue that needs you to stop. For a big lift or game, better injury-risk reduction is: - proper warm-up and gradual ramp-up sets - realistic load selection and good technique - sleep, hydration, and fueling - stopping for sharp, sudden, or worsening pain - addressing a recurring pain before competing/lifting through it If you need pain medicine just to participate, that’s worth discussing with a sports-medicine clinician, physio, or pharmacist—especially if this is frequent. And avoid routinely pre-dosing ibuprofen around hard exercise, particularly if dehydrated, fasting, drinking alcohol, or if you have kidney disease, ulcers/reflux, blood-pressure issues, or take blood thinners.
